---
// The yard's constraints (10 November): the throughput levers of the experiment yard, put where a
// shipwright would look for them. A shipyard is limited by its slipways, the tide, the timber store,
// the clerk, the jigs and the survey dock; so is this one. The table is deliberately about the yard,
// not about one ship: the question is how many voyages a week it can launch.
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { cardCls } from '../../lib/ui';
import YardConstraints from '../../components/islands/YardConstraints';
import ComputeResources from '../../components/islands/ComputeResources';
---
<Layout>
  <main>
    <YardConstraints client:load />
    <ComputeResources client:load />
    <p class="crumbs"><a href="/shipyard">← shipyard</a></p>
    <h1 class="yardtitle">The yard’s constraints <span class="q">a shipyard is not slow because its shipwrights are slow: it is slow because there are only so many slipways, the tide opens once, the timber must be seasoned, the clerk tallies every plank, the jigs are reset for every frame, and one survey dock holds one hull</span></h1>
    <section class={cardCls(false, 'panel')}>
      <p>These are the constraints on how many voyages this yard can launch, and the levers that move
        them. Each row is a constraint a shipwright would recognise; the two columns are the setting for
        a <b>300k</b> (a small hull, many of them) and a <b>1m</b> (a larger hull). The last column is
        what moving it buys the <i>yard</i> — not what it buys one hull.</p>
      <div class="scroll">
        <table class="selbooks yardtable">
          <thead><tr><th>the constraint</th><th>in the yard</th><th>300k</th><th>1m</th><th>what it buys</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><b>The tide</b></td>
              <td>how often a hull is hauled out for survey; miss the window and a day is lost</td>
              <td>light eval 64 → <b>256</b> steps; full 640 → <b>1,280</b></td>
              <td>light 64 → <b>128–256</b>; full 640 → <b>1,280</b></td>
              <td><b>2–4×</b> at 300k; less at 1m</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><b>The hold</b></td>
              <td>how much cargo one voyage carries: the batch</td>
              <td>32,768 → <b>65,536 or 131,072</b> tokens; scale lr and warmup</td>
              <td>32,768 → <b>65,536</b></td>
              <td><b>1.5–2×</b> at 300k; <b>1.3–1.8×</b> at 1m</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><b>The clerk</b></td>
              <td>the tally written for every plank: a ledger entry and a host sync at every step</td>
              <td>record every <b>10–50</b> steps; drop the per-step <span class="mono">.item()</span> / <span class="mono">.tolist()</span>; <span class="mono">async_eval</span></td>
              <td>the same</td>
              <td><b>5–15%</b></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><b>The jigs</b></td>
              <td>the templates the frames are cut from: compile the whole step, not only the backward pass</td>
              <td><span class="mono">step_fn</span> is compiled; clip, AdamW and the model update are outside it</td>
              <td>compile the optimizer and update too</td>
              <td><b>10–30%</b> at 1m</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><b>The rigging</b></td>
              <td>the stays and shrouds: don’t double every rope</td>
              <td>native GQA attention (<b>done</b>): 1 KV head for 2 query heads, no repeated K/V</td>
              <td>native GQA: 2 KV heads for 4 query heads</td>
              <td>small, free</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><b>The timber store</b></td>
              <td>how much seasoned wood is kept on hand: the MLX buffer cache</td>
              <td>4 GB → <b>1–2 GB</b>; two seats</td>
              <td><b>4 GB</b>; one seat</td>
              <td>less swapping, steadier steps</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><b>The slipways</b></td>
              <td>how many hulls can lie on the stocks at once</td>
              <td><b>2</b></td>
              <td><b>1</b></td>
              <td>throughput vs contention: four hulls on two slipways was four ships missing one tide</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><b>The survey dock</b></td>
              <td>the one dock every hull must pass: the light evaluations</td>
              <td>run them at the checkpoint or after a batch, not inside the loop</td>
              <td>low priority</td>
              <td>potentially <b>large</b> at 300k</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="note mt"><b>Read it as a yard, not a hull.</b> The 300k is a small hull: the tide and the
        survey dock bind first, and the model-level jigs barely matter. The 1m is a larger hull: the jigs
        and the rigging bind. Four hulls on two slipways did not make the yard faster; it made every hull
        miss the tide. A constraint is not a law: it is the thing the next change should move.</p>
    </section>
  </main>
</Layout>
